    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2035681931569107221",
      "url": "https://x.com/sciencekonek/status/2035681931569107221",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/sciencekonek/status/2035681931569107221",
      "text": "#SciFeature 𝗧𝗛𝗘 𝗕𝗨𝗦𝗬 𝗠𝗔𝗥𝗜𝗧𝗜𝗠𝗘 𝗥𝗢𝗨𝗧𝗘𝗦 🚢\n\nThe global oil crisis intensified when the Strait of Hormuz, a narrow waterway between the Arabian Peninsula and Iran, was effectively “closed” amid ongoing conflict. As a critical maritime choke point, it handles roughly 20% of the world’s oil supply, making disruptions globally significant.\n\nIn the Philippines, we also rely on busy sea lanes and strategic “choke points,” where trade and maritime traffic converge, making them equally vital and vulnerable.\n\nHere’s a map visualizing the bustling shipping routes within and surrounding the Philippine archipelago, transporting passengers and goods to and from the ports across the country. \n\nFrom ancient balangays to modern-day ferries and cargo ships, sea transport has always been the lifeblood connecting communities across our 7,000+ islands. Today, thousands of vessels navigate these waters daily, carrying passengers and goods that power our economy and link our lives.\n\nThis map shows the total AIS (Automatic Identification System) positions reported by commercial, fishing, oil & gas, passenger, and leisure vessels over a 6-year period, giving us a stunning view of both major and lesser-known maritime highways across the country. Notice how the Port of Manila stands out as the busiest hub, with vibrant routes also extending to and from key cities like Batangas, Cebu, and Cagayan de Oro.",
